      "headline": "Mexico appoints financier Roberto Lazzeri as ambassador to United States",
      "context": "Lazzeri, sent to lead Mexico's most difficult diplomatic front, discusses the future of the US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement and regional integration.",
      "background": "Mexico's president can appoint ambassadors without requiring political or diplomatic background, and Lazzeri's prior finance-ministry and development-bank roles put him in contact with the US Treasury and FinCEN during a 2025 money-laundering dispute involving three Mexican financial institutions.",
      "view": "Sheinbaum picking a financial technician over a career diplomat signals that the bilateral relationship's hardest problems now run through banking and compliance channels, not conventional diplomacy. That fits a moment when the review of the US-Mexico-Canada Agreement process moves to annual cycles after the US declined a 16-year extension in July 2026, and when disputes over money flows, like the border cash-transfer rule, are already straining the relationship outside trade policy.",
      "prediction": "Lazzeri's early tenure likely focuses on Treasury and FinCEN relationships rather than broad political outreach, given Sheinbaum's request that he engage the private and financial sectors. That would be wrong if he instead prioritizes congressional or US-Mexico-Canada trade agreement negotiating rounds over financial-sector diplomacy in his first 100 days.",
      "analysisV": 7,
      "drivers": "",
      "implications": "",
