      "headline": "China imposes preliminary 51.6 percent tariff on Mexican pecan exports",
      "context": "China's Ministry of Commerce imposed the tariff on Mexican pecan exports citing dumping. Mexico is working to block the measure, which threatens a key agricultural export.",
      "background": "Mexico exported about $9.1bn total to China in 2024 against $129.5bn in imports, so pecans are a rare Mexican export line into a market where Mexico normally buys, not sells.",
      "view": "The tariff matters less for Mexico's overall trade, since direct pecan exports to China were $21.9m in 2024, a rounding error next to the roughly $665bn in total Mexican goods exports in 2025 and dwarfed by the $872bn in US-Mexico goods trade. The real exposure is reexports through the United States, which the preliminary ruling explicitly targets by hitting US exporters with a higher 54.3% rate, suggesting China is trying to close a triangulation route rather than punish Mexican producers directly.",
      "prediction": "Mexico's economy ministry likely negotiates a reduced final rate before the investigation closes, since it has ten days to respond and is already coordinating with the Chinese embassy. That expectation fails if Beijing finalizes rates at or above the 51.6% preliminary level despite the talks.",
      "analysisV": 7,
      "drivers": "",
      "implications": "",
